## Finding

The immediate defect is in the existing product's filesystem lock guard, added
at accepted step 0129 (`3807ea3`), not in 0132's new attachment shapes.

The constructor TRX independently records 1,076 executed cases: 1,075 passed,
one failed, zero skipped. The failure is
`OpticalConstructor.Tests.KitProxyFolderTests.a lock left on this machine by a process that has ended is taken over`.
At `KitProxyTests.fs:144`, one of three simultaneous openers returned `KitIoErr`
with access denied to `.oc/lock-guard.tmp`; the existing assertion requires
`KitLockedErr` identifying the single winner. Sequential stale-lock takeover had
already succeeded before the contention call at line 984. All four prescribed
0132 attachment facts passed.

`KitFolderStore.fs:98-111` opens the guard with `OpenOrCreate`, exclusive sharing
and `DeleteOnClose`. It catches only `IOException` whose low HRESULT is 32 or 33.
Other failures escape into `tryKitFolderIo`, which retains only the exception
message in `KitIoErr`. Both acquisition and release call this shared guard and
recheck ownership after obtaining it. The current attachment additions do not
enter this code path; blame places the guard entirely in step 0129.

Build and elevation passed. The constructor suite failed with process exit 2;
the eleven s16 invariants were consequently not reached. A later successful
build does not replace the failed suite. The preceding full constructor runs at
0129, 0130 and 0131 passed with 1,058, 1,062 and 1,072 cases respectively. This
supports an intermittent contention defect, not a consistently broken setup.

## What this review adds

1. **The suspected native mechanism has a strong external precedent, but the
   exact incident remains uninstrumented.** Microsoft documents that opening a
   file pending deletion can return access denied. A .NET runtime issue contains
   a reproduction using the same `OpenOrCreate` / `FileShare.None` /
   `DeleteOnClose` combination and discusses the same intermittent exception.
   This supports the deletion-race hypothesis; it does not prove which exception
   type/HRESULT occurred in this test run. See [CreateFile documentation](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/fileapi/nf-fileapi-createfilea)
   and [.NET runtime issue 61395](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/issues/61395).

2. **A native error code alone cannot reliably distinguish transient deletion
   from a permanent permission denial.** The .NET discussion identifies that
   ambiguity. Capture exception type and HRESULT during reproduction, but do not
   make the repair depend on a supposedly unique transient access-denied code.
   A small, finite retry policy confined to opening this coordination file can
   tolerate the transient case and still return a real `KitIoErr` for a persistent
   failure. It must never invent a winning holder. [Runtime discussion](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/issues/61395)

3. **The existing sharing-violation retry is already unbounded.** `tryOpen`
   recurses after every 32/33 failure with `SpinWait` and no deadline or attempt
   cap. That is a separate, directly visible hang risk. The repair should bound
   the whole guard-open retry, not merely add access denial to its catch filter.
   This also applies to release, which uses the same guard.

4. **ASG behaved as designed in this incident.** The worker received an explicit
   instruction to repair within declared effects or report an authority conflict.
   Slice 0132 permits only Domain `Kit.fs` and Tests `KitTests.fs`; the measured
   failure needs Storage `KitFolderStore.fs` and related existing lock tests in
   `KitProxyTests.fs`. Judge cycle 2 and the one allowed resolver confirmed that
   conflict. This is not another exhausted repair loop or a missing-terminal-event
   defect. There were no QUOTA_HIT events in this launch; the prior auth fix is
   not implicated. No ASG source change or budget increase is justified here.

5. **The receipt and census remain valid.** Read-only validation reports no
   receipt errors and no temporal replay findings. The four attachment identities
   are the four authorized additions. This is an actual test failure, unlike the
   earlier undeclared-identity/count issues. Keeping the existing lock fact
   identities avoids another census amendment.

The primary diagnosis and its proposed shared-guard repair are sound. I refine
its recovery sequence below so the operator can commit the completed repair
normally, using an explicit measured-checkpoint exception instead of requiring
